.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt NFPCAPD 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nfpcapd
.Nd packet-capture to NetFlow daemon
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl i Ar interface
.Op Fl r Ar file
.Op Fl s Ar snaplen
.Op Fl B Ar cachesize
.Op Fl e Ar active,inactive
.Op Fl I Ar ident
.Op Fl C Ar config
.Op Fl w Ar flowdir
.Op Fl F Ar filter
.Op Fl p Ar pcapdir
.Op Fl H Ar host[/port]
.Op Fl m Ar socket
.Op Fl S Ar num
.Op Fl t Ar interval
.Op Fl P Ar pidfile
.Op Fl D
.Op Fl d
.Op Fl u Ar userid
.Op Fl g Ar groupid
.Op Fl o Ar option[,option]
.Op Fl z=lzo
.Op Fl z=bz2
.Op Fl z=lz4[:level]
.Op Fl z=zstd[:level]
.Op Fl W Ar num
.Op Fl x Ar key=value
.Op Fl K Ns Oo = Ns Ar passphrase | Pf @ Ar keyfile Oc
.Op Fl k Ns Oo = Ns Ar passphrase | Pf @ Ar keyfile Oc
.Op Fl V
.Op Fl h
.Op Ar '<filter>'
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the pcap capture daemon of the nfdump tools. It reads network
packets from an interface or from a file and directly creates nfdump
records. Nfdump records are written either locally to a directory in
the same format as nfcapd, or can be forwarded to a nfcapd collector
somewhere else in the network.
.Nm
is nfcapd's packet-capture counterpart, shares many options, and generates the same type of files.
.Nm
likewise creates, rotates and stores files. See also
.Xr nfcapd 1
for more information on common option.
.Pp
In 1.8.x, offline pcap input is processed in batches using mmap where
available; gzip input uses a batch-copy path. Packet decoding is a
self-contained state-machine pipeline, and native pcap output avoids an
unnecessary conversion step.
.Pp
.Nm
optionally also stores pcap traffic data in separate files and
uses the same rotation interval as the NetFlow data. Storing PCAP traffic data
files is only possible locally.
.Pp
.Nm
is multithreaded and uses separate threads for packet, netflow
and pcap processing.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl i Ar interface
Listen on this interface in promiscuous mode for packet processing.
.It Fl r Ar file
Read and process packets from this file. This file must be PCAP-compatible.
.It Fl s Ar snaplen
Limit the snaplen on collected packets. The default is 1522 bytes. The
snaplen needs to be large enough to process all required protocols. The
snaplen must not be smaller than 54 bytes.
.It Fl B Ar cachesize
Sets the initial node cache size. By default the cache size is set to 8192 nodes, and grows and shrinks dynamically.
.It Fl e Ar active,inactive
Sets the active and inactive flow expire values in seconds. The default is 300,60.
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
Active timeout: A flow gets flushed to disk after this period even if it is still active. As a rule of thumb, it should correspond with the
.Fl t
rotation value, in order to reflect continuous traffic in the flow files.
.It
Inactive timeout: A flow gets flushed to disk after being inactive for this number of seconds. It frees up node resources.
.El
On busy networks these values can be set to more aggressive timeouts.
.It Fl I Ar ident
Specifies an ident string describing the source, for example the name of the interface or host. This string is put into the stat record to identify the source. Default is 'none'. Same as nfcapd(1).
.It Fl C Ar config
Read runtime configuration from
.Ar config .
The value
.Dq none
disables configuration-file loading. See
.Xr nfdump.conf 5 .
.It Fl w Ar flowdir
Specifies the base directory to store the flow files.
If a sub hierarchy is specified with
.Fl S
the final directory is concatenated to
.Ar base_directory/sub_hierarchy .
.It Fl p Ar pcapdir
Optionally stores network packets in PCAP-compatible files in this directory and rotates files at the same time as the flow files. Subhierarchy directories are applied likewise.
.It Fl H Ar host[/port]
Send nfdump records to a remote
.Xr nfcapd 1
collector via UDP. Default port is 9995. Flows are not stored locally.
The wire format used is nfdumps binary protocol v251 for plain or XChaCha20-Poly1305 encrypted records.
Use
.Fl k
to enable encrypted transport.
The file-compression options
.Fl z
do not apply for
.Fl H .
Records are compressed (zstd if available, otherwise LZ4)
before sending, independent of
.Fl k .
The target wire packet size (1200 bytes by default) can be raised further via
.Sy udp.sendThreshold
in
.Xr nfdump.conf 5 ,
useful on paths known to carry a larger MTU end to end (jumbo frames,
tunnels, loopback) for an additional reduction in packet count.
.It Fl F Ar filter
Apply a post-decode record filter before flows are stored
.Pq Fl w
or forwarded
.Pq Fl H .
Non-matching records are discarded.
.Ar filter
uses the same filter syntax as
.Xr nfdump 1 ,
restricted to plain record element filters such as
IP address/network/list, port, protocol, byte/packet counters, TCP flags, VLAN,
MPLS, AS number, engine/exporter fields, time ranges, and similar direct
comparisons or boolean combinations thereof.
Filter primitives based on record enrichment
.Pq geo country lookup, Tor exit-node lookup, timezone lookup, and payload content matching
are rejected with an error at filter-compile time.
.It Fl m Ar socket
Send collector flow-summary metrics to the specified UNIX socket.
.It Fl S Ar num
Allows to specify an additional directory sub hierarchy to store the data files. The default is 0, no sub hierarchy, which means the files go directly in the base directory (
.Fl w ).
The base directory (
.Fl w )
is concatenated with the specified sub hierarchy format to form the final data directory. For a full list of hierarchies see nfcapd(1).
.It Fl t Ar interval
Specifies the time interval in seconds to rotate files. The default value is 300 seconds (five minutes). The smallest interval is 2 seconds. The intervals are synchronized with the wall clock.
.It Fl P Ar pidfile
Specify name of pidfile. Default is no pidfile.
.It Fl D
Daemon mode: fork to background and detach from terminal.
.Nm
terminates on signal TERM, INT and HUP.
.It Fl d
De-duplicate packets with a window size of 8. This option is useful if packets in a PCAP file are duplicated. If packets on multiple span ports are sent to the collector, they are de-duplicated within the window size. Note: de-duplication operates from the IP layer to the end of the packet, so layer-1 and VLAN/MPLS headers are stripped.
If used together with
.Fl p ,
the resulting pcaps are de-duplicated as well.
.It Fl v
Set verbose level. Accepted 0 to 4. The default level is 1 with moderate information. Use -v 3 to
print NetFlow records in raw format while collecting. Please note that not all elements
are printed. This option is for debugging purposes only, to verify that incoming NetFlow data is processed correctly.
.It Fl u Ar userid
Change to the user
.Ar userid
as soon as possible. Only root is allowed to use this option. Uid/Gid is switched after opening the reading device.
.It Fl g Ar groupid
Change to the group
.Ar groupid
as soon as possible. Only root is allowed to use this option. Uid/Gid is switched after opening the reading device.
.It Fl o Ar option[,option]
Adds options to nfpcapd. Two options are available:
.Bl -item -compact
.It
fat     Add MAC addresses, optional VLAN and MPLS labels.
.It
payload   Add the payload bytes of the first packet of a connection.
.El
.It Fl z=lzo
Compress flows. Use fast LZO1X-1 compression in output file.
.It Fl z=bz2
Compress flows. Use bz2 compression in output file. Note: not recommended while collecting.
.It Fl z=lz4[:level]
Compress flows. Use LZ4 compression in output file.
.It Fl z=zstd[:level]
Compress flows. Use zstd compression in output file.
.It Fl K Ns Oo = Ns Ar passphrase | Pf @ Ar keyfile Oc
Encrypt output flow files (local storage) using ChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD authenticated encryption.
Requires
.Nm
to be compiled with libsodium support.
.Fl K
encrypts each rotated output file with a distinct per-file derived key.
.Pp
The passphrase may be supplied in one of three ways:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl K Ns = Ns Ar passphrase
Use
.Ar passphrase
directly.
The
.Ql =
is required; there must be no space between
.Fl K
and the passphrase.
.It Fl K Ns Pf @ Ar keyfile
Read the passphrase from the first line of
.Ar keyfile .
Trailing newlines are stripped.
This avoids exposing the passphrase in the process table.
.It Fl K
(no argument) Prompt interactively from the terminal with echo disabled.
.El
.Pp
The encryption key is derived from the passphrase using Argon2id
.Pq Li crypto_pwhash
with a freshly generated random per-file salt, so every rotated output file uses a distinct
derived key.
Encrypted files can be read and decrypted with
.Xr nfdump 1
using the same
.Fl K
option.
.It Fl k Ns Oo = Ns Ar passphrase | Pf @ Ar keyfile Oc
Enable authenticated encryption for UDP flow transport when forwarding to a remote collector
.Pq Fl H
using XChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD authenticated encryption (nfd wire format v251).
Requires
.Nm
to be compiled with libsodium support.
.Fl k
derives a 32-byte session key from the passphrase via Argon2id and sends flows
as protocol version 251.
The receiving
.Xr nfcapd 1
must be started with the same passphrase via
.Fl k .
.Pp
The passphrase may be supplied in one of three ways:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl k Ns = Ns Ar passphrase
Use
.Ar passphrase
directly.
The
.Ql =
is required; there must be no space between
.Fl k
and the passphrase.
.It Fl k Ns Pf @ Ar keyfile
Read the passphrase from the first line of
.Ar keyfile .
Trailing newlines are stripped.
.It Fl k
(no argument) Prompt interactively from the terminal with echo disabled.
.El
.It Fl W Ar num
Set the core limit to
.Ar num
CPU cores.
The number of writer threads used to compress and write flow files is derived
automatically from this budget and the chosen codec.
Setting
.Ar num
to 0 (the default) uses all available online CPU cores.
If
.Ar num
exceeds the number of online CPU cores, a warning is logged and the budget
is clamped to the available core count.
.Pp
The writer count can be overridden per-run with
.Fl x Ar threads.writers=N
(0 restores the auto-derived count) or via the
.Em [threads]
section in
.Xr nfdump.conf 5 .
.It Fl x Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
Override a single configuration key for this run only.
May be repeated to override multiple keys.
See
.Xr nfdump.conf 5
for the list of available keys and
.Xr nfdump 1
for a full description of the override mechanism.
.It Fl V
Print nfpcapd version and exit.
.It Fl h
Print help text to stdout with all options and exit.
.It Ar '<filter>'
Optional pcap compatible packet filter. The filter needs to be put within quotes.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
returns 0 on success, or 255 if initialization failed.
.Sh LOGGING
.Nm
logs to syslog with SYSLOG_FACILITY LOG_DAEMON.
For normal operation level 'error' should be fine.
More information is reported at level 'info'.
.Pp
A small statistic about the collected flows, as well as errors are reported at the end of every interval to syslog with level 'info'.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Read packets from interface eth0:
.Pp
.Dl nfpcapd -i eth0 -z=zstd -D -w /netflow/flows -S 2 -I any -P /var/run/nfpcapd.pid
.Pp
Read packets from interface mx0 and store also packets in pcap files:
.Pp
.Dl nfpcapd -i vmx0 -z=lz4 -D -w /netflow/flows -p /netflow/caps
.Pp
Send records to a remote host:
.Pp
.Dl nfpcapd -i eth1 -H 192.168.200.10/12344 -D -e 60,20 -o fat,payload
.Pp
Send records using encrypted native forwarding (libsodium build):
.Pp
.Dl nfpcapd -i eth1 -H 192.168.200.10/9995 -k@/etc/nfdump/forward.key
.Sh NOTES
.Nm
can store records either locally or send it to a remote host but not both at the same time.
.Pp
When records are sent to a remote
.Xr nfcapd 1
process,
.Nm
uses nfdump native forwarding protocol v251 for plain or with
.Fl k encryption.
Both endpoints must run compatible nfdump 1.8.x versions. The transport uses
network byte order and does not require matching host endianness.
.Pp
The flow cache is checked in regular maintenance batches.
It expires flows according to the expire values.
The batch interval defaults to 5 seconds and can be set with
.Dq flowcache.expireinterval
in the
.Nm
configuration section or with
.Fl x Ar flowcache.expireinterval Ns = Ns Ar seconds .
It accepts 1 through 60 seconds and must not exceed the shorter active or
inactive timeout.
All elapsed time-wheel slots are processed, so changing this value affects
expiry latency, memory residency, and output batching but not expiry
correctness.
Expired flows are flushed and processed and nodes are freed up.
.Pp
The flow cache defaults to a maximum of 262144 nodes (about 100 MiB of node
metadata) and 64 MiB of retained packet payload.
Set
.Dq flowcache.max_nodes
and
.Dq flowcache.max_payload_bytes
to change these budgets.
Set the payload budget to zero to disable retained payloads.
The packet-to-flow output queue is bounded by
.Dq flowcache.max_output_nodes
(65536 by default).
When it is full, completed flow records are dropped and reported in the final
queue statistics; rotation and shutdown control records wait for space.
.Pp
A smaller snaplen may improve performance, but may result in loss of information.
The smallest snaplen of 54 bytes can process regular TCP/UDP/ICMP packets. In case of Vlan or MPLS labels, not enough information may be available for correct protocol decoding. Nfdump records may be incomplete and set to 0.
.Pp
If IP packets are fragmented, they are reassembled before processing. All IP fragments need to be reassembled in order to be passed to the next stage. If not all fragments are correctly assembled within 15s since the first fragment arrived, all fragments are discarded.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nfcapd 1 ,
.Xr nfdump 1 ,
.Xr nfexpire 1
.Sh BUGS
No software without bugs! Please report any bugs back to me.
